dcnet for unixshell
===================

This directory is the unixshell package form of the dcnet project. It is built
from the top-level dcnet/ tree by:

    python tools/build_dcnet_pkg.py

Layout
------

    manifest              package metadata (name, version, bin=dcnet)
    bin/dcnet.lua         the command: master / worker / screen / status / jobs / net
    lib/dcnet/*.lua       dcnet modules, loaded by bin/dcnet.lua
    jobs/*.lua            the built-in example jobs
    share/worker.lua      packaged worker program (seed for the master's push)
    etc/dcnet.conf.example  copy to /etc/dcnet.conf and edit

Install
-------

Copy this directory into a package store as /usr/pkgs/dcnet (or publish it from
a pkgserver repository and use "pkg install dcnet"). The shell finds the
command through the package manifest and lib/ from the store's module path.

Usage
-----

    dcnet master                 start the master console
    dcnet master --headless      run the master as a log-only daemon
    dcnet worker [ID] [NAME]     start a worker console
    dcnet worker --headless      run the worker as a log-only daemon
    dcnet screen [MONITOR]       network screen: draw the master panel on a monitor
    dcnet status                 state root, jobs and recent history
    dcnet cmd <line>             queue a command for the running master
    dcnet log [N]                last N lines of the master log
    dcnet jobs                   list the jobs directory
    dcnet net                    show transport and modems
    dcnet config                 show the resolved configuration

Configuration is /etc/dcnet.conf (see etc/dcnet.conf.example). The writable
state root defaults to /var/lib/dcnet for root and $HOME/.dcnet otherwise.

boot=master|worker  starts that role before the first ush prompt. On advanced
computers multishell=1 (the default) puts dcnet in tab 1 and ush in tab 2;
multishell=0 falls back to a headless background job. forward=1 makes the shell
a relay: every line is written to the master's control queue and executed there;
"dcnet log" reads the result.

Compute nodes can use the smaller image built by "python tools/build_worker.py":
it contains only this package's worker modules and a startup that loops
"dcnet worker --headless" (no ush, no fonts, no interactive UI).

For a machine that should boot straight into a role, point /etc/session.conf at
the command (unixshell runs it through ush before the first prompt):

    command=dcnet worker --headless

